            },
            "reviewBody": "\"salad dressing\" usually refers to Miracle Whip, not just any random dressing."
          },
          {
            "@type": "Review",
            "reviewRating": {
              "@type": "Rating",
              "ratingValue": "5"
            },
            "author": {
              "@type": "Person",
              "name": "Daniel Pigg"
            },
            "reviewBody": "We like ours very creamy so we used 4 tblsp of miracle whip , 1tsp of mustard, and a 1/.2 tsp 50/50 salt pepper mix I use for BBQ. I also like paprika. There very yummy. Tip for boiling eggs do your self a favor and get an instapot we used 5-5-5 and the shells practically fall off the egg with no scaring of the egg and the yokes are creamy but cooked.",
            "datePublished": "2018-03-03T04:48:33.277Z"
          },
          {
            "@type": "Review",
            "reviewRating": {
              "@type": "Rating",
              "ratingValue": "5"
            },
            "author": {
              "@type": "Person",
              "name": "GoldSyrup3475"
            },
            "reviewBody": "Miracle Whip makes the best devilled eggs! Forget mayo!! I use more Miracle Whip, about 3-4 tablespoons for 8 eggs. I always get compliments--they're the best! [An important tip, though: Use a fork and pulverize the egg yolks before adding anything else! Filling will be smooth and creamy, with NO lumps!]",
            "datePublished": "2024-11-17T05:24:54.673Z"
          },
          {
            "@type": "Review",
            "reviewRating": {
              "@type": "Rating",
              "ratingValue": "4"
            },
            "author": {
              "@type": "Person",
              "name": "KarenAndKevin"
            },
            "reviewBody": "I made this and it was great! I did make a few adjustments though. I used 9 eggs (that's what my tray holds), 3 tablespoons of mayonnaise (instead of miracle whip), 1 1/2 teaspoons yellow mustard, and I added 1/8 teaspoon of garlic powder. It's a hit!!",
            "datePublished": "2017-04-22T14:05:48.083Z"
          },
          {
            "@type": "Review",
            "reviewRating": {
              "@type": "Rating",
              "ratingValue": "5"
            },
            "author": {
              "@type": "Person",
              "name": "Baker12"
            },
            "reviewBody": "I loved these! I made these deviled eggs as a taste tester and got great reviews form my family. When the recipe says \"salad dressing\" you should actually use mayo. ( not miracle whip) : ) and you do not need to use prepared mustard, any mustard works. Tip, put the ingredients in a small bowl and then mix until creamy. Then, put it in a plastic bag, cut the tip off and squeeze the mixture into the egg!",
            "datePublished": "2018-04-18T01:57:13.100Z"
          },
          {
            "@type": "Review",
            "reviewRating": {
              "@type": "Rating",
              "ratingValue": "4"
            },
            "author": {
              "@type": "Person",
              "name": "I luv veggies"
            },
            "reviewBody": "I added 1-2 tsp sweet pickle relish. Delicious! I also used real mayo! LOVED IT! Will definitely make this again!",
            "datePublished": "2020-12-22T11:59:17.440Z"
          },
          {
            "@type": "Review",
            "reviewRating": {
              "@type": "Rating",
              "ratingValue": "4"
            },
            "author": {
              "@type": "Person",
              "name": "Chillonice"
            },
            "reviewBody": "Added more miracle whip, salt, pepper, and a little bit of relish. Really tangy, and perfect texture.",
            "datePublished": "2020-01-19T19:58:13.500Z"
          },
          {
            "@type": "Review",
            "reviewRating": {
              "@type": "Rating",
              "ratingValue": "5"
            },
            "author": {
              "@type": "Person",
              "name": "Sarah Cunningham"
            },
            "reviewBody": "I substituted horseradish sauce for the mayo. They were very flavorful.",
            "datePublished": "2020-01-06T05:23:58.257Z"
          },
          {
            "@type": "Review",
            "reviewRating": {
              "@type": "Rating",
              "ratingValue": "5"
            },
            "author": {
              "@type": "Person",
              "name": "Mark Fowler"
            },
            "reviewBody": "If you use mayo, then you are missing the point of this recipe. This is kind of a southern way of making them (Miracle Whip is a must). Go ahead and cave in to the mayo if you wish but the sweetness will be missing. Now my mother, whose from the south, adds sugar to taste in addition to the Miracle Whip. This is the basic ingredient list but mom added the sugar also and tasted while she went. Later she added Splenda as a sweetener but I would use sugar myself, not too much. Her church loved them and one man asked for her deviled eggs on his death bed in the hospital. People have loved her eggs for over 50 years. Every church she attended would always ask her to bring them to pot lucks/events. Escape the mayo folks :-)",
            "datePublished": "2025-11-19T15:29:24.275Z"
          },
          {
            "@type": "Review",
            "reviewRating": {
              "@type": "Rating",
              "ratingValue": "5"
            },
            "author": {
              "@type": "Person",
              "name": "Duke"
            },
            "reviewBody": "This is a great basic recipe! I use Hellman's mayo instead of Miracle Whip, and add a little pickle relish and 1 tsp vinegar",
            "datePublished": "2025-07-05T18:37:01.028Z"
          },
